## Goal
Show how the six samples of this project (A1-A3 untreated, B1-B3 treated) relate to each
other in gene-expression space: a principal component analysis of the gene count matrix, with
the sample points coloured by condition. The plot is for seeing whether samples separate by
condition, and which samples sit apart; no test or interpretation is part of this analysis.

## Inputs
Artifact types resolve through each sub-stage's OUTPUTS.tsv at execution time; the paths
below are what they resolve to today.

| Artifact type | Resolved from | Path |
|---|---|---|
| `counts_gene` | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper` (native) | `02_bioinformatics/rnaseq_bulk/01_nfcore-rnaseq-wrapper/run/results/star_salmon/salmon.merged.gene_counts_length_scaled.tsv` |

Sample condition comes from the design table `00_data/rnaseq_bulk/samples.csv` (columns
`sample_id`, `condition`), joined to the matrix columns by sample ID. Both are read-only.

## Method
Environment: `gars-bio` (pandas, numpy, scikit-learn, matplotlib; nothing is installed).
All steps run in one script, `scripts/sample_pca.py`, submitted as one batch job.

1. Read the `counts_gene` TSV (genes x samples; `gene_id`, `gene_name`, then one column per
   sample). Use the sample columns only; check they match the `sample_id` values in
   `samples.csv` exactly, and stop with an error if any sample is missing from either side.
2. Drop genes with zero counts in every sample. No other filtering of samples.
3. Transform: `log2(x + 1)`. The matrix is salmon length-scaled gene counts (non-integer), so
   this is a plain log transform, not a variance-stabilising transform.
4. Select the 500 most variable genes (variance across samples of the log2 values); if fewer
   than 500 genes remain, use all of them and record the number used.
5. PCA with `sklearn.decomposition.PCA` on the samples x genes matrix (columns mean-centred,
   not scaled to unit variance), all components (at most 5 for 6 samples), `svd_solver="full"`.
6. Write the PC scores per sample (with condition) and the variance explained per component.
7. Plot PC1 vs PC2, one point per sample, coloured by condition, each point labelled with its
   sample ID, axes labelled with percent variance explained. Save as PNG (300 dpi) and SVG.
   Colours are a colour-blind-safe two-colour pair; condition is also distinguished by marker
   shape so the plot survives greyscale.

